.TH DMEVENTD 8 "DM TOOLS #VERSION#" "Red Hat, Inc." \" -*- nroff -*-
.
.de NSY
.  ie t .SY \\$1
.  el \{\
.    ie \\n(.$>1 \{\
.      PD 0
.      TP \\w'\\$1\ 'u
.    \}
.    el .TP
.    B \\$1
.    nh
.    na
.  \}
..
.
.SH NAME
.
dmeventd \(em Device-mapper event daemon
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.
.NSY dmeventd 1
.RB [ -d
.RB [ -d
.RB [ -d ]]]
.RB [ -e\ \c
.IR exit_on_path ]
.RB [ -f ]
.RB [ -g\ \c
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ -h ]
.RB [ -i ]
.RB [ -l ]
.RB [ -R ]
.RB [ -V ]
.RB [ -? ]
.
.PD
.ad
.hy
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.
dmeventd is the event monitoring daemon for device-mapper devices.
Library plugins can register and carry out actions triggered when
particular events occur.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.
.TP
.B -d
Repeat from 1 to 3 times
.RB ( -d ,
.BR -dd ,
.BR -ddd )
to increase the detail of
debug messages sent to syslog.
Each extra \fBd\fP adds more debugging information.
.
.TP
\fB-e\fP \fIexit_on_path\fP
Specifies a file path.  When the daemon receives a signal (SIGINT, SIGTERM),
the presence of this file allows the daemon to exit even
if there are still monitored devices.
This can help with system shutdown where devices
have not been unmonitored properly.
To disable this behavior set this to the empty string "".
Default value is "\fI#DEFAULT_DMEVENTD_EXIT_ON_PATH#\fP".
.
.TP
.B -f
Don't fork, run in the foreground.
.
.TP
\fB-g\fP \fIseconds\fP
Set the grace period for monitoring thread cleanup (0-300 seconds, default: 10).
During this period, monitoring threads that have finished processing events
will wait for potential reuse rather than immediately terminating.
If the same device and plugin combination is registered again within
the grace period, the existing thread is reused, significantly reducing
overhead. Set to 0 to disable thread reuse.
.
.TP
.B -h
Show help information.
.
.TP
.B -i
Query the running daemon instance for the status information. The format is
internal and unstable and it is targeted for developers.
Format may change between versions.
.
.TP
.B -l
Log through stdout and stderr instead of syslog.
This option works only with the -f option; otherwise it is ignored.
.
.TP
.B -?
Show help information on stderr.
.
.TP
.B -R
Replace a running dmeventd instance. The running dmeventd must be version
2.02.77 or newer. The new dmeventd instance will obtain a list of devices and
events to monitor from the currently running daemon.
.
.TP
.B -V
Show version of dmeventd.
.
.SH LVM PLUGINS
.
.TP
.B Mirror
Attempts to handle device failure automatically.
See
.BR lvm.conf (5).
.
.TP
.B Raid
Attempts to handle device failure automatically.
See
.BR lvm.conf (5).
.
.TP
.B Snapshot
Monitors how full a snapshot is becoming and emits a warning to
syslog when it exceeds 80% full.
The warning is repeated when 85%, 90% and 95% of the snapshot is filled.
See
.BR lvm.conf (5).
A snapshot that runs out of space becomes invalid and when it is mounted,
it is unmounted if possible.
.
.TP
.B Thin
Monitors how full the thin pool's data and metadata are becoming and emits
a warning to syslog when either exceeds 80% full.
The warning is repeated when more than 85%, 90% and 95%
of the thin pool is filled. See
.BR lvm.conf (5).
When a thin pool fills over 50% (data or metadata), the thin plugin calls
the configured \fBdmeventd/\:thin_command\fP with every 5% increase.
With the default setting it calls internal
\fBlvm lvextend --use-policies\fP to resize the thin pool
when it exceeds the configured threshold
\fBactivation/\:thin_pool\%_auto\%extend\%_threshold\fP.
If the command fails, the dmeventd thin plugin will keep
retrying execution with an increasing time delay between
retries, up to 42 minutes.
The user may also configure an external command to support more advanced
maintenance operations of a thin pool.
For example, the external command can remove unneeded snapshots,
use \fBfstrim\fP(8) to recover free space in a thin pool,
or use \fBlvextend --use-policies\fP if other actions
have not released enough space.
The command is executed with the environment variable
\fBLVM_RUN_BY\%_DM\%EVENTD=1\fP
so any lvm2 command executed in this environment
will not try to interact with dmeventd.
To see the fullness of a thin pool, the command
may check these two environment variables
\fBDMEVENTD\%_THIN\%_POOL\%_DATA\fP and
\fBDMEVENTD\%_THIN\%_POOL\%_METADATA\fP.
The command can also read status with tools like \fBlvs\fP(8).
.
.TP
.B Vdo
Monitors how full a VDO pool's data is becoming and emits
a warning to syslog when it exceeds 80% full.
The warning is repeated when more than 85%, 90% and 95%
of the VDO pool is filled. See
.BR lvm.conf (5).
When a VDO pool fills over 50%, the vdo plugin calls
the configured \fBdmeventd/\:vdo_command\fP with every 5% increase.
With the default setting it calls internal
\fBlvm lvextend --use-policies\fP to resize the VDO pool
when it exceeds the configured threshold
\fBactivation/\:vdo_pool\%_auto\%extend\%_threshold\fP.
If the command fails, the dmeventd vdo plugin will keep
retrying execution with an increasing time delay between
retries, up to 42 minutes.
The user may also configure an external command to support more advanced
maintenance operations of a VDO pool.
For example, the external command can free up unused space
with \fBfstrim\fP(8),
or use \fBlvextend --use-policies\fP
if other actions have not released enough space.
The command is executed with the environment variable
\fBLVM_RUN_BY\%_DMEVENTD=1\fP so any lvm2 command executed
in this environment will not try to interact with dmeventd.
To see the fullness of a VDO pool, the command may check the
\fBDMEVENTD_VDO_POOL\fP environment variable.
The command can also read status with tools like \fBlvs\fP(8).
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.
.TP
.B DMEVENTD_THIN_POOL_DATA
Variable is set by the thin plugin and is available to the executed program.
Value represents actual usage of thin pool data volume.
Variable is not set when error event is processed.
.
.TP
.B DMEVENTD_THIN_POOL_METADATA
Variable is set by the thin plugin and is available to the executed program.
Value represents actual usage of thin pool metadata volume.
Variable is not set when error event is processed.
.
.TP
.B DMEVENTD_VDO_POOL
Variable is set by the vdo plugin and is available to the executed program.
Value represents actual usage of VDO pool data volume.
Variable is not set when error event is processed.
.
.TP
.B LVM_RUN_BY_DMEVENTD
Variable is set by the thin and vdo plugins to prohibit recursive interaction
with dmeventd by any lvm2 command run from a thin_command or vdo_command
environment.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.
.nh
.na
.BR lvm (8),
.BR lvm.conf (5),
.BR lvextend (8),
.P
.BR fstrim (8)
